/-
# Groups with {3, 5}-involutions (Kourovka Notebook Problem 19.36)

Kourovka Notebook, Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 19.36 (E. Jabara): for a non-empty set `D` of
odd integers greater than `1`, a periodic group `G` is a group with `D`-involutions if it is
generated by its involutions and, for involutions `x, y`, the order of `xy` lies in `{1, 2} ∪ D`
with all these values occurring. Conjecture: a group with `{3, 5}`-involutions is isomorphic to `A₅`
or to `PSU(3, 4)`.
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.FieldTheory.Finite.GaloisField
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.SpecificGroups.Alternating
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.Torsion

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.ThreeFiveInvolutionsKourovka19Problem36Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 19.36 (E.
Jabara). UnsolvedMath record UM-00701584 (`KOU-19.36`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

Periodicity (`IsMulTorsion G`) is kept as in the problem, although Bettio proves the conjecture
without it. "All these values actually occur" is read for all of `{1, 2, 3, 5}`, as in Bettio's
abstract ("with all possibilities occurring"). Mathlib has no projective special unitary group, and
its `Matrix.unitaryGroup` needs a star structure, which `GaloisField 2 4` does not carry; MathlibExt
has none either, so `PSU(3, 4)` is defined here: `PSU(3, 4) = SU₃(4) / Z(SU₃(4))`, where `SU₃(4)`
consists of the matrices `A ∈ GL₃(𝔽₁₆)` (`𝔽₁₆ = GaloisField 2 4`) of determinant `1` with `Āᵀ A =
1`, `Ā` applying the involution `x ↦ x⁴` of `𝔽₁₆` entrywise; all non-degenerate Hermitian forms over
`𝔽₁₆` are equivalent, so this is the standard unitary group (of order 62400, with trivial centre).
`A₅` is `alternatingGroup (Fin 5)`.
-/

/-- `x` is an involution: `x² = 1` and `x ≠ 1`. -/
def IsInvolution {G : Type*} [Group G] (x : G) : Prop :=
  x ^ 2 = 1 ∧ x ≠ 1

/-- The special unitary group `SU₃(4)`: the matrices `A ∈ GL₃(𝔽₁₆)` of determinant `1` with
`Āᵀ A = 1`, where `Ā` applies the involutive field automorphism `x ↦ x⁴` of `𝔽₁₆` entrywise (the
isometries of the Hermitian form `x₁ȳ₁ + x₂ȳ₂ + x₃ȳ₃`). Written as the subgroup generated by these
matrices, which already form a subgroup. -/
noncomputable def specialUnitaryGroupThreeFour : Subgroup (GL (Fin 3) (GaloisField 2 4)) :=
  Subgroup.closure {A | Matrix.transpose ((A : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) (GaloisField 2 4)).map
      (· ^ 4)) * (A : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) (GaloisField 2 4)) = 1 ∧
    (A : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) (GaloisField 2 4)).det = 1}

/-- The projective special unitary group `PSU₃(4) = SU₃(4) / Z(SU₃(4))`. -/
abbrev PSUThreeFour :=
  specialUnitaryGroupThreeFour ⧸ Subgroup.center specialUnitaryGroupThreeFour

/-- Kourovka 19.36 (E. Jabara): a periodic group `G` with `{3, 5}`-involutions, that is, generated
by its involutions, with the order of `xy` in `{1, 2, 3, 5}` for all involutions `x, y` and each
of these four values attained, is isomorphic to `A₅` or to `PSU(3, 4)`. -/
def threeFiveInvolutionsClassification : Prop :=
  ∀ (G : Type) [Group G], IsMulTorsion G →
    Subgroup.closure {x : G | IsInvolution x} = ⊤ →
    (∀ x y : G, IsInvolution x → IsInvolution y → orderOf (x * y) ∈ ({1, 2, 3, 5} : Set ℕ)) →
    (∀ d ∈ ({1, 2, 3, 5} : Set ℕ), ∃ x y : G, IsInvolution x ∧ IsInvolution y ∧
      orderOf (x * y) = d) →
    Nonempty (G ≃* alternatingGroup (Fin 5)) ∨ Nonempty (G ≃* PSUThreeFour)

/--
Resolved true: Bettio proved Jabara's conjecture, even without the hypothesis that `G` is periodic:
a group generated by involutions in which the product of two involutions has order 1, 2, 3 or 5, all
four occurring, is isomorphic to `A₅` or to `PSU(3, 4)`; the Kourovka Archive records the proof.
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 19.36 (E.
Jabara); resolution: E. Bettio, A proof of a conjecture by Jabara on groups in which all involutions
are odd transpositions, J. Group Theory 24 (2021), no. 5, 1055-1067, abstract: a group generated by
elements of order two in which the product of any two such elements has order 1, 2, 3 or 5, with all
possibilities occurring, is isomorphic to A₅ or to PSU(3, 4), answering Problem 19.36; Kourovka
Archive, Problem 19.36: the conjecture is proved, even without using the hypothesis that G is
periodic, https://doi.org/10.1515/jgth-2020-0129.
-/
public theorem_wanted threeFiveInvolutionsClassification_holds : threeFiveInvolutionsClassification

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.ThreeFiveInvolutionsKourovka19Problem36Wanted
